From 9810dd16e2ddae477b93f9d6e14598a08de58a80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 09:21:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Fail a quota-walled Desktop turn over to the next
 route in its class, whatever the wall's length

A Desktop chat: three turns failed before the agent was built
(SubscriptionQuotaUnavailable anthropic/claude-fable-5-2, resets later that day) and the user saw only "Your request was not
processed", while claude-opus-5-5 in the same deep class ran the same chat fine. The earlier automated
deliveries to this chat hit the same wall.

Five whys: the turn refused -> the build raised the wall -> may_reroute() held any wall resetting under 6 h
(WALL_REROUTE_MIN_SECONDS, "waiting beats a provider switch") -> that rule was written for 33 h Codex walls and
priced a refusal at nothing -> a turn refused for an hour is a lost turn for a person and a lost notice for an
automation, and the router-picked pin is not the user's choice anyway.

Fix: may_reroute() drops the 6 h floor. A non-explicit pin whose wall has a known future reset moves within its
class (same-class-failover with walled_from, one chat notice naming the reset) at any length. Explicit pins
(a switch, a named model) and Kanban card pins still hold, as do key/endpoint overrides and sessionless builds.
Desktop turns, the messaging gateway and automated wakes share the one resolver, so all three take it. A held
wall still closes as the route_refused card naming the reset in PT; Desktop now also hands the refused words back
to the composer for route_refused (refused-sends), like a busy-chat refusal.

RED: six tests in tests/agent/test_walled_default_pin.py fail on main (short wall, half-pool short wall, Desktop
build, launch-key log) and pass here; 52 routing/wall test files (959 tests) green.
